From: Termux Launcher <noreply@pathayam.xyz>
Subject: [PATCH] syscall: answer close_range with ENOSYS

Android's app seccomp filter on older releases (Android 12 among them) does
not list close_range, and a disallowed call is answered with SIGSYS, which
kills the process. Bun, inside Claude Code and opencode, calls it through
syscall() during start-up. ENOSYS is what a kernel older than 5.9 says, and
callers already fall back from it (closing descriptors one by one), so the
programs start everywhere; the fallback costs next to nothing on releases
that would have allowed the call.

--- a/src/misc/syscall.c
+++ b/src/misc/syscall.c
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include "syscall.h"
 #include <stdarg.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 
 #undef syscall
 
@@ -17,5 +18,9 @@
 	e=va_arg(ap, syscall_arg_t);
 	f=va_arg(ap, syscall_arg_t);
 	va_end(ap);
+	/* Older Android releases (12 among them) kill a process for close_range:
+	 * the seccomp filter they put on every app predates it. Answer as an
+	 * older kernel would, so callers take their fallback. */
+	if (n == SYS_close_range) return __syscall_ret(-ENOSYS);
 	return __syscall_ret(__syscall(n,a,b,c,d,e,f));
 }
